<content>
  <section><title>Activity (5 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity gives students a quick exposure to the inequalities \(x&gt;0,\;x\geq0,\;y&gt;0,\;and\;y\geq0,\) so that they are prepared to deal with them later in this lesson. It also reinforces the idea of thinking carefully about whether the points on the boundary lines of a solution region are included in the solution set.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Is the ordered pair (5.43, 0) a solution to all, some, or none of these inequalities? Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(x &gt; 0\)</item>
  <item>\(y &gt; 0\)</item>
  <item>\(x\;\geq\;0\)</item>
  <item>\(y\;\geq\;0\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Some. (5.43, 0) is a solution to \(\;x&gt;0,\;x\geq0,\) and \(y\geq0\). It is not a solution to \(y&gt;0\).</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>

<div class="os-raise-usermessage-lightbulb">
  <para>Tip: Remind students that the upper-right region of the coordinate plane is called the first quadrant.</para>
</div>

<para>Invite students to share their responses. Then, display a blank four-quadrant coordinate plane for all to see. </para>
<para> <media><image alt="Blank x y coordinate plane.&#10; " src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/44e5c41f2c2ceb7faca155b36b03bf44511d3969"/></media></para>
<para><emphasis>Use the following questions to lead a class discussion. </emphasis></para>
<list>
  <item>"If we were to graph the solutions to \(x&gt;0\), what would the region look like?" (We would shade the right side of the \(y\)-axis.) "Is the \(y\)-axis included in the solution region?" (No)</item>
  <item>"What about the graph of the solutions to \(y&gt;0\)?" (We would shade the upper side of the \(x\)-axis). "Is the \(x\)-axis included in the solution region?" (No)</item>
  <item>"What about the graph of the solutions to the system \(x&gt;0\) and \(y&gt;0\)?" (The solution region would be the upper-right section of the graph, where the other two regions overlap.)</item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
